#. Tag: para
#: tool-geolocationeditor.docbook:5
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"The Geolocation Editor allows for adding and editing GPS coordinates of "
"picture metadata. The geo-data is stored into the image (EXIF tags) and the "
"location can be displayed in an external browser map."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: keyword
#: tool-geolocationeditor.docbook:11
#, no-c-format
msgid "<keyword>GPS</keyword>"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: keyword
#: tool-geolocationeditor.docbook:12
#, no-c-format
msgid "<keyword>Map</keyword>"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: tool-geolocationeditor.docbook:16
#, no-c-format
msgid "Geolocation Editor"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: tool-geolocationeditor.docbook:18
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"The <quote>Geolocation Editor</quote> provides the tools to localize "
"pictures geographically in the common spherical coordinate system (used by "
"GPS/Galileo). Altitude, latitude and longitude are used."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: tool-geolocationeditor.docbook:23
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"The tool only works for image formats that have EXIF or XMP metadata "
"supported by <ulink url=\"http://www.exiv2.org\">Exiv2 library</ulink>."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: tool-geolocationeditor.docbook:28
#, no-c-format
msgid "Geolocation"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: tool-geolocationeditor.docbook:29
#, no-c-format
msgid "The tool has two methods to mark the pictures with coordinates:"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: tool-geolocationeditor.docbook:34
#, no-c-format
msgid "Batch correlation of GPS tracking data with a series of images."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: tool-geolocationeditor.docbook:37
#, no-c-format
msgid "Semi-automatic coordinate attribution to single pictures."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: tool-geolocationeditor.docbook:41
#, no-c-format
msgid "The correlator"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: tool-geolocationeditor.docbook:42
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"In order to correlate your images with geographic data you need to have a "
"GPS tracking information available as a XML file in <literal>gpx</literal> "
"format (<application><ulink url=\"http://www.gpsbabel.org\">gpsbabel</"
"ulink></application> and <application><ulink url=\"http://www.ncc.up.pt/"
"gpsman/\">gpsman</ulink></application> can download and convert tracking "
"data from a GPS device for you)."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: tool-geolocationeditor.docbook:48
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"<menuchoice><guimenu>Image</guimenu> <guimenuitem>Geolocation</guimenuitem> "
"<guimenuitem>Correlator</guimenuitem></menuchoice>. This interface can "
"correlate a previously selected number of pictures with registered GPS "
"tracking information."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: tool-geolocationeditor.docbook:56
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"The geo-correlation dialog based on EXIF date/time and tracking data (gpx "
"format)"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: tool-geolocationeditor.docbook:59
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Select the images you want to correlate in the application main view, then "
"call the menu. The above dialog will show up. To indicate possible time/"
"location correlation you have to load a track file with <guilabel>Load GPX "
"File</guilabel> that contains GPS data taken at the same time and location "
"as the pictures with your camera."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: tool-geolocationeditor.docbook:66
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"When the file is loaded, an automatic correlation is done showing the "
"attributed coordinates in red. GPS track data is invariably recorded in GMT "
"time, so you need to match the camera time with GMT, which can be done with "
"<guilabel>Time Zone</guilabel>. Select the <emphasis>camera time zone</"
"emphasis> here!"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: tool-geolocationeditor.docbook:73
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"The <guilabel>Max. time gap</guilabel> setting specifies the limit within "
"which GPS time and camera time shall be deemed coincident. The maximum value "
"is 2000 seconds."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: tool-geolocationeditor.docbook:78
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"If you have no tracking data for pictures that were taken in the same place "
"you can just manually edit a <literal>gpx</literal> file and apply it to "
"those images. The 2000 second time gap will then correlate all pictures "
"taken within 20 minutes."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: tool-geolocationeditor.docbook:84
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"The option <guilabel>Interpolate</guilabel> and <guilabel>Difference in min."
"</guilabel> can be used if there is no match of data. If you know that in "
"reality there is a location match despite a larger time gap between camera "
"and GPS, you can set this limit here and click on the <guilabel>Correlate</"
"guilabel> button to interpolate."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: tool-geolocationeditor.docbook:92
#, no-c-format
msgid "Correlation"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: tool-geolocationeditor.docbook:95
#, no-c-format
msgid "240 minutes is the maximum time difference that can be introduced here."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: tool-geolocationeditor.docbook:101
#, no-c-format
msgid "Edit Coordinates"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: tool-geolocationeditor.docbook:103
#, no-c-format
msgid "Semi-automatic attributing of coordinates through map interaction"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: tool-geolocationeditor.docbook:106
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"The coordinates can be set manually with this dialog. This comes in handy if "
"there is a series of photos taken at the same location. Since the "
"coordinates are memorized from one dialog call to another, it is as easy as "
"clicking on <guilabel>OK</guilabel> to set the previous data to the current "
"photo."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: tool-geolocationeditor.docbook:113
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Otherwise the location can be found and fixed iteratively with the displayed "
"map. Click on the region of interest, zoom in, adjust location by clicking "
"again, and so on until sufficient accuracy is achieved. The altitude must "
"always be entered manually. The map can be switched between map mode, "
"satellite image or mixed mode."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: tool-geolocationeditor.docbook:122
#, no-c-format
msgid "Commandline tagging"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: tool-geolocationeditor.docbook:123
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Completely outside of the &digikam; context, the are ways to GPS tag images. "
"Here is a shell script that uses <command>exiftool</command> to tag a batch "
"of images."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: programlisting
#: tool-geolocationeditor.docbook:130
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"#!/bin/sh\n"
"# exiftool wrapper script for easy commandline use. It treats regex files or "
"whole directories.\n"
"#\n"
"#                        FMT                  Output\n"
"#                \"%d deg %d' %.2f\"\\\"    54 deg 59' 22.80\"\n"
"#                \"%d deg %.4f min\"      54 deg 59.3800 min\n"
"#                \"%.6f degrees\"         54.989667 degrees\n"
"\n"
"if [ -z $1 ]; then \n"
"   echo \"Usage: exiftool-gps-wrapper lat long [alt] file || dir (use signed "
"floating coordinates)\"\n"
"else\n"
"\n"
"   if  [ -z \"$4\" ]; then  foo=\"$3\"; alt=0\n"
"   else                          foo=\"$4\"; alt=$3\n"
"   fi\n"
"\n"
"   echo $foo\n"
"   if [ -e \"$foo\" ] ; then\n"
"        latR=$(echo \"$1\" | awk '{if ($1 &lt; 0) print \"S\"; else print \"N"
"\"}')\n"
"        lonR=$(echo \"$2\" | awk '{if ($1 &lt; 0) print \"W\"; else print \"E"
"\"}')\n"
"        lat=$(echo \"$1\" | awk '{val = $1; if ($1 &lt; 0) sub(/-/, \"\", "
"val); print val}') # absolute value\n"
"        lon=$(echo \"$2\" | awk '{val = $1; if ($1 &lt; 0) sub(/-/, \"\", "
"val); print val}') # absolute value\n"
"        # use the following syntax for easy googlian paste of "
"ll=-1.23456,53.345345\n"
"        #lonR=$(echo \"$2\" | awk '{if ($2 &lt; 0) print \"W\"; else print "
"\"E\"}')\n"
"        echo $lat, $latR, $lon, $lonR, $3, $4, $foo\n"
"        \n"
"        function setgpsinfo ()\n"
"        {\n"
"            exiftool -c \"%.6f\" -GPSMapDatum=\"WGS-84\" -GPSAltitude=$5 \\\n"
"            -GPSLongitudeRef=$4 -GPSLongitude=$3 -GPSLatitudeRef=$2 -"
"GPSLatitude=$1 \"$6\"\n"
"            exiftool -GPS:ALL \"$6\"\n"
"        }\n"
"\n"
"        if [ -d \"$foo\" ] ; then\n"
"\n"
"            for i in \"$foo\" ; do    # the selection of files treated "
"depend on the passed regex\n"
"                echo \"i= \"$i\n"
"                setgpsinfo $lat $latR $lon $lonR $alt \"$i\"\n"
"            done\n"
"        else\n"
"            setgpsinfo $lat $latR $lon $lonR $alt \"$foo\"\n"
"        fi\n"
"   else        echo \"file or folder is wrong\"\n"
"   fi\n"
"fi"
msgstr ""

